  To mitigate these effects, research in machine learning suggests detecting and handling outliers promptly.
  Although important, designing anomaly detection methods is challenging, especially for non-linear models and high-dimensional data.
  In response, this paper explores the theoretical requirements for detecting outliers in statistical models, focusing on non-parametric and linear models. 
  Using conceptually simple proofs, the research demonstrates that appropriate marginal densities are necessary conditions for constructing anomaly detectors. 
  The authors also present a novel perspective on the purpose of outlier detection, viewing it as estimating the feature region that meets a certain model standard rather than identifying unusual observations. 
  The study outlines several implications of this perspective, including the observation that the best detector often corresponds to the native marginal density estimator of the model used. 
  The research further contributes several practical findings, such as the effectiveness of using native marginal densities from Gaussian models and the ineffectiveness of using native marginal densities from discrete models. 
  The paper concludes by providing guidance on picking appropriate outlier detection methods based on the statistical properties of the models employed. 
  The research utilizes self-contained proofs, making it accessible to readers without advanced mathematical backgrounds. 
  The paper includes three figures, four algorithms, one theorem, and five lemmas to facilitate understanding. 
  The authors propose future research directions on combining outlier detection with optimization problems and using deep learning models.  Bookmark and Share
